## Chapter 1.3: The Charred Body

Luka pieced together the fragmented headlines and morning bulletins lingering in his memory, trying to recall the person in the picture. Finally, he got an answer.

“Igor Romashin… Is he the director of logistics from Khabarovsk? The one accused of embezzlement a few days ago?”

“Yup, exactly,” Nadezhda nodded then chuckled. “Heh, I hope you didn’t just use your vision on me. You do know quite a lot, Comrade Huo—maybe more than you should,” she teased, lightly drumming her fingers against the wooden counter.

Luka returned a smile before saying, “Well, to answer your question: No, I haven’t seen him.”

She nodded knowingly, already half-expecting the answer. While she could bid farewell right now, she hesitated. Internally, her professionalism and her instinct bickered. What she wanted to say was confidential, but there was something about Luka that she found _special_. Perhaps his intellectual authority and quiet earnestness made him more trustworthy in her eyes—or perhaps she was simply dying to share the burden of a secret.

“In that case, you’ve missed your chance…” She led him on, knowing that his curiosity would want to know more.

“What do you mean?” He replied, as expected.

She leaned in close over the glass partition, whispering, “If I tell you this, you have to swear to keep it a secret, okay?”

“Uh… are you sure about this? If this is confidential, I’d rather not—”

“Yes, it’s confidential and it’s terribly unprofessional, but sooner or later the whole town will hear about it anyway.”

Luka wanted to refuse, but his curiosity wouldn’t let him. What could have been so important that this officer wanted to break protocol to tell him?

“…Fine. Let’s hear it.”

Nadezhda took a moment to paraphrase the news, glancing left and right toward the empty hallway. “This morning, Igor Romashin was found dead in his sports car, burned to a crisp. Right here in Pogranichny.”

Luka was taken aback. A high-profile fugitive ended up dead in this town? Burned to a crisp, no less. In a peaceful socialist era where violent crime was an absolute statistical anomaly, homicide was something people rarely ever had to think about.

“…I— Wow. I mean, when did this happen?” Luka asked the only question that came to his mind.

“We don’t know for sure, but a citizen reported a fire around 4:30. I wasn’t on duty at the time,” which was a formal way of saying that she was fast asleep under her warm blanket, “the first responders along with firefighters arrived at the scene, put out the fire, and discovered the body.”

“But that’s all I heard,” she looked to the side with disappointment in her eyes, slumping back in her swivel chair. “It sucks missing out on all the action and sitting quietly here. Imagine it, even a patrol officer got a sneak peek! Ugh, this is killing me, comrade. Out of all the times it could have happened, it just has to be on my shift…” She let out a big, dramatic sigh that ruffled the bangs on her forehead.

Luka was caught in the awkwardness; he couldn’t just get up and leave. She was venting to him not as an officer to a citizen, but as a comrade to a comrade. He didn’t quite know how to comment since he had no clue how the Militsiya worked, but as an academic who frequently navigated lab schedules, talking about shifts, he might know a thing or two.

“Um… couldn’t someone cover your shift for you?” Luka quietly suggested.

“Covering my shift, huh…” Nadezhda pondered. That thought hadn’t crossed her mind because she didn’t want to be penalized this early in her career. But hearing it out loud, she realized it might just work.

“Yeah. Yeah! I think that’s a great idea, Comrade Huo!” 

She quickly turned to her terminal, tapping fast on the clattering keyboard. Her smirk widened every second, like a mastermind seeing their plan fit together. Luka could only sit there on the bench, wondering if he had just accidentally enabled a junior officer to go rogue.

A moment later, the magnetic coupler chimed. She snatched a freshly synced sheet of e-paper from the output slot and rolled it neatly in her hand. “Now, comrade, we just have to wait for the final piece…” She smiled mischievously.

Luka raised his eyebrow in confusion. Nadezhda was definitely more of a wild card than he’d imagined. As he was about to ask what she meant, she cut him off.

“And… here comes the devil,” she leaned to the right to look past Luka toward the entrance. 

Luka looked back and saw a senior officer stepping through the heavy storm doors. As he turned back, Nadezhda said to him, “Okay, wait here a moment,” then stood up and walked gleefully right over to greet the older officer.

“Good morning, Uncle Senya!” She saluted with a bright smile.

A silver-haired senior officer, probably in his late 40s to early 50s, stomped the Siberian snow from his heavy valenki[footnote:fg5hd] and slowly took off his thick winter coat, revealing his uniform underneath. The insignia on his shoulder boards bore the broad, golden-yellow longitudinal stripe of a veteran Starshina[footnote:fvtrp].

%Footnote.fg5hd: _Valenki_ (Валенки [ˈvalʲɪnkʲɪ]): Traditional seamless Russian winter boots crafted from pressed sheep's wool felt. Highly insulating in extreme sub-zero weather, they are standard cold-weather issue across Siberian and Far Eastern districts.

%Footnote.fvtrp: _Starshina_ (Старшина [stərʂɨˈna]): The highest non-commissioned officer (NCO) rank in the Soviet military and Militsiya, equivalent to a First Sergeant or Master Sergeant. Distinct from academy-graduated commissioned officers, Starshinas are career veterans who serve as the precinct's administrative anchors, logistical managers, and station supervisors.

“To you too, Nadya,” he saluted back in a gravelly, grandfatherly voice. He knew that attitude all too well—the hyper-activeness, the unnaturally friendly smile. It was clear that she wanted something. “So… What can I do for you, lass?”

“Have you been to Igor’s crime scene, Uncle?” She asked.

Senya gazed upward, scratching his chin through his ushanka[footnote:fd6c9] as he tried to remember, “Igor? Crime scene?” It seemed he was oblivious to the recent events.

%Footnote.fd6c9: _Ushanka_ (Ушанка [ʊˈʂankə]): The iconic Soviet winter fur cap with folding ear-flaps (_ushi_). In sub-zero Far Eastern border conditions, the flaps are tied beneath the chin to guard against severe windchill.

“Oh. I thought you knew,” she tried to explain concisely, keeping her voice low. “Well, Igor is that high-profile fugitive from Khabarovsk. We found him dead this morning, burned to a crisp―”

“Woah, woah, hold on right there! Don’t fill an old man's head with gory images before breakfast! It’ll ruin my morning tea.” Senya stopped her story. He wasn’t very fond of death.

“Ah, sorry, Uncle!” she tilted her head slightly with an apologetic grin, “but let me get to the point. Could you cover for me for just an hour, Uncle?” She gave him the pleading eyes of the youth.

“Eh… but my shift doesn't start until noon. What’s the grand occasion?” Senya hesitated, not wanting to agree just out of whim.

“I’ve never seen a real crime scene before, Uncle! Please… I’ll bring you back whatever you want from town. Snacks, pastries, fidget toys, whatever you like!” Nadezhda went full throttle on her plea, clasping her hands together.

Senya let out a hearty chuckle. “Alright, alright. A senior like me ought to support an aspiring youth after all. Just don’t take too long now,” he approved. “And also… get me an oolong tea,” he smirked.

“Thank you, Uncle Senya!” She saluted him again and trotted back to Luka.

Luka overheard the entire conversation. It seemed he shouldn’t underestimate how scheming she was; given the chance, she would bend every protocol if it got her what she set her mind to.

“That went better than expected,” she said, standing there proud of what she had achieved. “Now, Comrade Huo, want to tag along? Provided you're free from your academic duties, of course.”

Luka thought, “_Oh great, now she’s just going to keep dragging me around like her partner-in-crime,_” but a seed of curiosity was already planted inside him. Besides, what else was he going to do for the rest of the day anyway? He had already called in sick at PIRM, his wormhole calculation was already done, and it wasn't like he was in the mood to go drink with Alex.

“…Well, since I already called in sick at PIRM…” He tried to sound reluctant, not wanting to look like a total pushover.

Her eyes lit up. Since Luka arrived, her day just kept getting better and better. “Perfect! Let me grab my winter coat.” She scuttled around the desk to fetch her gear.

Senya, who had just walked behind his side of the desk, cradling a steaming glass mug of tea, glanced briefly at Luka, then turned to Nadezhda and teased, “Well, well, Nadya… since when did you get yourself a boyfriend?”

Nadezhda froze mid-button, rolled her eyes lightly, and let out a small chuckle. “Ugh, Uncle, I’ve told you a hundred times that I like women, haven’t I?”

“I don’t know, maybe you woke up and realized you were bisexual, haha!” he laughed. Nadezhda shook her head with an exasperated groan to dismiss his silly hypothesis.

During that time, Luka buttoned his winter jacket and pulled his wool scarf tight, getting ready to head out into the cold Siberian winter again.

“All set, comrade. Follow me!” She walked ahead to the door, leading the way.

“Good luck out there!” Senya shouted from behind the desk. “Don't let Tarasov bite your heads off!”

Luka and Nadezhda looked back and nodded before pushing through the double storm doors into the biting April wind.
